To efficiently delete every third element of a given linked list while preserving the order of the remaining elements, we can use a two-pointer approach. We will keep two pointers, `curr` and `next`, where `curr` points to the current node, and `next` points to the next node. We will iterate through the list and skip over every third element by advancing `next` to the node after the third element. Once we reach the end of the list, we will set the `next` pointer of the last node to `NULL`, effectively deleting the last node.

Here is the algorithm:
```
void deleteThirdElement(Node* head) {
    Node* curr = head;
    Node* next = head->next;

    while (curr != NULL) {
        if (curr->data % 3 == 0) { // if the current node is a multiple of 3
            // advance next to the node after the third element
            next = next->next;
        } else {
            // set the next pointer of the current node to the next node
            curr->next = next;
        }

        // move the pointers to the next node
        curr = next;
        next = next->next;
    }

    // set the next pointer of the last node to NULL
    curr->next = NULL;
}
```
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the list. We only visit each node once, and each operation takes constant time.

The space complexity of this algorithm is O(1), since we only need to keep track of two pointers, which take constant space.

Note that this algorithm modifies the original list, so it is not suitable for scenarios where the original list needs to be preserved. If the original list needs to be preserved, we can create a new list and copy the non-deleted nodes to the new list.

