To perform a clockwise rotation of a matrix in-place, we can use the following algorithm:
```
function rotateClockwise(matrix) {
  // Get the number of rows and columns in the matrix
  const rows = matrix.length;
  const cols = matrix[0].length;

  // Iterate over each row and column
  for (let i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
      // Calculate the new position of the element
      const newRow = cols - j - 1;
      const newCol = rows - i - 1;

      // Swap the elements at the old and new positions
      const temp = matrix[i][j];
      matrix[i][j] = matrix[newRow][newCol];
      matrix[newRow][newCol] = temp;
    }
  }

  return matrix;
}
```
The above algorithm works by iterating over each row and column of the matrix, and swapping the elements at the old and new positions. The new position is calculated by switching the row and column indices and subtracting 1 from each of them. This ensures that the elements are rotated in a clockwise direction.

Here's an example usage of the `rotateClockwise` function:
```
const A = [[1,2,3],
           [4,5,6],
           [7,8,9]];

console.log(rotateClockwise(A));
// Output: [[3,6,9],
//          [2,5,8],
//          [1,4,7]]
```
Note that this implementation assumes that the input matrix is square, meaning that the number of rows and columns is the same. If the matrix is not square, the algorithm will still work, but the resulting matrix may have a different shape.

